00:01:36.037 --> 00:01:37.370
STEREN GIANNINI: Thank you, Jay.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.920
So what is serverless?

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.170
You might have attended some
other sessions about it.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.070
Let me recap today the
few characteristics

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:50.150
that we use to define serverless
products on Google Cloud.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.520
The first one is that
a serverless product

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.850
is for developers.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.500
Users of A serverless
product focus

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.950
on delivering business
value, focus on code.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.770
The provider takes
care of the rest.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.750
This is a key characteristic.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.660
The second one is
that it scales.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.620
Its scales to zero, which
means if you don't use it,

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.560
it will slowly get down to zero.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.840
But if you use it a lot,
potentially it scales with you.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.240
So really, there
is no provisioning

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:28.980
to do in advance, no management
of number of instances.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.280
This is done for
you automatically.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.930
The third characteristic
is that it is managed.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:41.140
So by managed, of course,
the Cloud provider

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:45.340
is taking care of the
infrastructure for you,

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.080
is taking care of the updates
of this infrastructure,

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.490
of the reliability of
this infrastructure,

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.570
but also of the security.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:58.840
And we provide a lot of security
features for our serverless

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.990
products out of the box.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:05.020
So managed security, too.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.810
The last point is
a billing model.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:12.280
You really pay for what you use.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.520
So because we have
this operational model,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.250
where it scales up
and down following

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.840
your traffic, your needs,
we are able to provide you

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.930
a billing modeled where you
only pay when you use it.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:28.950
So as a consequence,
you don't need

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.860
to commit to a certain
usage in advance.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:37.320
You do not need to provision
certain cluster size.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:37.820
No, no, no.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.090
It's all based on what you need
at a certain point in time,

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.710
and you will only pay for that.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.750
If you take those
characteristics,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:51.500
you can see how it applies to
more than just small snippets

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.320
of code in the Cloud,
more than just functions.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.300
If you take those
definitions, you

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.470
can see that we can apply
these to many areas,

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:05.030
like database, like storage,
like AI, like data processing.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.150
All of these, Google
Cloud has products

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.990
that we qualify to be
serverless because they

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:15.630
answer so those points.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.110
So you can see on
the data storage

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.050
side, one product
I really recommend

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:22.970
that is a great
pair with serverless

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.120
compute is a Cloud Datastore.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.880
On the file storage side we
have Cloud Storage, PubSub,

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.010
Cloud ML Engine, BigQuery.

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.980
All of these you do not have to
care about the infrastructure.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:36.390
You really pay when you use it.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.590
So all of these are what
we qualify serverless.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:45.660
For this talk, we are going to
focus on serverless compute.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.620
So today all 2G [INAUDIBLE]
available products

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.100
in Google Cloud that
are serverless compute

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.290
are Cloud functions
that we announced

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:56.790
to be generally
available yesterday,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.980
and App Engine that has
been here for a while

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.730
and loved by many,
many customers.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:06.670
So these are all serverless
compute products.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:11.470
And recently we
introduced a newcomer

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:15.510
in this picture, which is
the GKE Serverless Add-On.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:20.860
So to be clear, the
GKE Serverless Add-On

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:25.060
allows you to get a serverless
developer experience

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.630
within your own
Kubernetes cluster.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:31.780
But it does not provide you
with the serverless economics.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:33.820
However, all of the
other attributes

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.470
are available for this product.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:39.730
So it has been announced today
and is currently in alpha.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.364
So this is a serverless compute
picture of Google Cloud.

00:05:46.364 --> 00:05:48.280
Now I'm going to help
you a little bit picking

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.220
between these because I can
acknowledge that this might not

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.700
be easy sometime.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:57.790
And the first one, Cloud
Functions, as the name

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.809
says, the unit is the function.

00:06:00.809 --> 00:06:02.350
So here you are
going to really think

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.800
about not something
that does many purposes,

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.190
but only one thing.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:12.180
So it's really well tailored for
single purpose microservices.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.000
And because you can trigger
a function not only via HTTP

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.720
but also via events,
it will be a good fit

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.800
when you want to do asynchronous
workloads or things that

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.970
react after all something
in your Cloud architecture

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:29.230
has triggered.

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:32.470
So everything that is
event-based and async

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.710
are a good use case
for Cloud Functions.

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:38.740
Regarding event triggers, we
keep adding more and more.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.402
To mention a few, you
can trigger a function,

00:06:44.402 --> 00:06:46.360
of course, when there is
an HTTP request coming

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:52.152
in, but also for certain
logs for PubSub events,

00:06:52.152 --> 00:06:54.610
when there is a database change,
like recently we announced

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:57.790
the availability of
Cloud file store events

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:00.400
for Google Cloud Functions.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.750
You can trigger a function when
there is a new file in Cloud

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:06.730
storage, so really
many, many events,

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.570
and we are going to keep
adding more all the time.

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:12.940
This is Cloud Functions.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:15.780
The second product I
mentioned is App Engine.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:20.190
So App Engine, the unit
here is an application.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:26.010
So an application will be source
code that you deploy and deploy

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.810
to App Engine.

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:29.760
The workloads that you
deploy to App Engine

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.970
are triggered via HTTP.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:37.480
So that means that HTTP
coming from the outside world,

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.270
so if, for example,
you are going

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:45.510
to create a mobile API or just
a website with a front end

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:49.830
and multipurpose websites, then
App Engine is a very good fit.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.550
Also, those HTTP requests
coming from the inside.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:55.620
So you might have an
architecture with many services

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.410
and services talking
to each other,

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.510
and they obviously can
use HTTP to do that.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:05.200
So App Engine would be best used
when you migrate an existing

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.400
code base because, as
we've announced yesterday,

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:13.020
we now have what we call the
App Engine second generation run

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:13.770
times.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:16.560
And those are what
we say idiomatic.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:17.520
So what does that mean?

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.390
That you can take any module
in your favorite programming

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.670
language and bring
them on App Engine.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.350
And it's all serverless.

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.030
If you are a Node.js
developer, you

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:31.870
can use any modules
from NPM, be it

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:34.110
a regular one or a native
one, like something

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:36.570
that uses binary code.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:39.480
You can do it on App
Engine standard now.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.990
We announced recently the
availability of Python 3

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:44.810
and PHP 7 on App
Engine standard.

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:48.330
So also something that really
scales down to zero and scales

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.520
with you to snapshot scale.

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:53.200
App Engine has been
here for a while.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:54.690
But if you take
the characteristics

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:57.270
of what a serverless
service is, App Engine

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:00.320
is serverless, App
Engine standard.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.490
And the last newcomer is
the GKE Serverless Add-On.

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:08.000
So here the unit is
the container image.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.420
So of course the add-on allows
you to build from source

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.000
to image, but at the end
of the day, the things that

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.170
are running and scaled for
you are container images,

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:21.040
even if you built
them from source using

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:23.090
[INAUDIBLE] tooling.

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:24.980
So when would you pick that one?

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:28.520
You would pick it when you're
coming from a Kubernetes world

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.280
and you want to give
to your developers

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:35.150
in your organization a
serverless experience.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.895
Developers should not have to
worry about pods and replica

00:09:37.895 --> 00:09:40.190
sets and scaling those pods.

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:40.970
No.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:44.560
They just want to deploy
code, and they want things

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.570
like the system to
handle the rest.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:50.620
And this system is what the
serverless add-on gives you.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:54.370
So you take it when you
already have a GKE cluster,

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:56.410
when you want to stay in it.

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:58.060
You would pick
that when you have

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:02.140
some special needs of resources
that App Engine or Cloud

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.070
Functions do not give
you, so for example,

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:08.042
custom VM sizes or you
want to access GPU.

00:10:08.042 --> 00:10:09.500
Or you will pick
that, for example,

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:12.190
when you want to be close
to another workload that

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:14.020
is already in GKE.

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:17.800
So let's assume you have an
elasticsearch service running

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:18.940
on GKE.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:23.660
Then if you want your serverless
workloads to be close to it,

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:26.000
then this will be a good fit.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.570
So the GKE Serverless Add-On
is currently in alpha,

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:32.301
but all of what we are going to
talk about later apply to it,

00:10:32.301 --> 00:10:34.630
too.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:39.080
So this is a picture of the
serverless in Google Cloud.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.710
However, there are
some challenges

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:43.820
monitoring serverless.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:48.370
The first one will be that by
definition, the servers are not

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:49.760
accessible.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.170
So that means a few things.

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:55.060
That means that for App
Engine and Cloud Functions,

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:56.560
you don't control the OS.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:59.650
So if you, for example, have
to install a particular agent,

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:01.780
monitoring agent, you can't.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:04.720
So that presents a challenge.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:08.830
Also, in the past we just
SSH'd into an instance

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:09.970
to see what's wrong.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:13.600
Well, you can't in Cloud
functions or App Engine.

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:15.940
This is not something
we give you.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:18.770
You cannot just
SSH the usual way.

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:23.550
So we'll see how we provide
tools to address that.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:28.620
Then, serverless encourages
to break the monolith

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.110
into independently
scalable pieces,

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:34.800
because obviously if those
pieces can scale up and down

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:38.910
to zero, then breaking
them into pieces

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:42.360
will be better
economics for you.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:46.439
If each of them can scale to
zero when you don't need them,

00:11:46.439 --> 00:11:47.980
that's what you
would be looking for.

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:50.880
So as a consequence, serverless
encourages microservices.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.600
And microservices mean
that you have more pieces

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.120
to monitor and
operate and to look,

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.020
to trace between all of these.

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:02.480
So we'll see how we
can help with that.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:05.000
Another point is auto scaling.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.610
As I said, a serverless
product scales with you.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.840
So when you don't have any
requests, there is no instance,

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:14.690
so there is nothing
to look, to monitor.

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.330
And when the traffic
comes in, then instances

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:19.640
are scheduled for you, and
then automatically they

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:22.320
are stopped when it's needed.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:25.910
Also, you cannot rely on the
fact that an instance will be

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:26.840
here a long time.

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:31.970
No, instances are spin up
and stopped very frequently

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:32.762
in serverless.

00:12:32.762 --> 00:12:33.720
So what does that mean?

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:38.780
That means that first,
you really cannot look

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:40.410
at the instance level.

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:43.740
You really should look
at the service level.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:47.060
The second is that
cold starts matter.

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:50.570
As you know, if instances
are started and stopped very

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.510
frequently, then you cannot do
a lot of things at startup time.

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.750
So you cannot load many
modules, many monitoring agents,

00:12:56.750 --> 00:12:59.030
when things start.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:02.670
And for functions, it's
even going one step further,

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:05.300
which is that in
Cloud functions,

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:08.360
outside of the execution
of the request,

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:09.990
you cannot do anything.

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:14.660
So that means that you cannot
expect to have CPU outside

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:19.060
of the request
response time frame.

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:21.170
So that's our third challenge.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:25.130
And lastly,
serverless, as I said,

00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:28.550
allows you to build
asynchronous workloads.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:31.590
Here, for example, you
can react to an event.

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:35.120
You can use a queue
to enqueue some tasks.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:39.260
We have Cloud tasks that we
released to beta recently.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:41.960
You can use PubSub
topics to send events

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:43.700
across your microservices.

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:48.960
So all of this makes it harder
to see what triggered what

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:51.230
in your microservices.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:55.700
So we will see later
how Stackdriver can help

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:58.400
you address those challenges.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.860
Jay?

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:02.900
JAY JUDKOWITZ: Thanks, Steren.

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:08.090
So like I said, Stack driver
is really, really fortunate

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:12.390
to be a part of Google,
where we have at our disposal

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:17.840
the whole SRE team to learn from
to see how they built Google

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:19.100
to where it is.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:22.250
And did everybody see the
book that they released

00:14:22.250 --> 00:14:25.080
and Revision 2
that just came out?

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.700
So I definitely encourage that.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.480
It's really good reading.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.280
So what did we learn from SRE?

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:35.070
And how are we taking
advantage of that?

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:40.100
So Google has eight services
with a billion or more users.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:41.951
We have 2,500 SREs.

00:14:41.951 --> 00:14:43.700
And the math doesn't
really work this way,

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:48.140
but that's over 400,000
users served per SRE, which

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:50.660
is incredible economy of scale.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:54.200
And not only that, the
SREs have a great culture

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:55.470
and love their work.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:58.530
And so this is a thing that
customers have come and said.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:02.120
Hey, we'd like to be
able to have a great ops

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:07.320
culture that scales and really
takes care of our customers.

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:14.040
Now to do this, they've
had to solve really

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:15.660
one huge trade-off.

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:18.000
On the one hand, reliability.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.600
Google.com is sort of
synonymous with my internet up.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.250
It's pretty reliable.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:26.400
But on the other
hand, they're trying

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:30.420
to figure out how to
keep Google engineers as

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:31.960
productive as possible.

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:32.910
How can we experiment?

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:33.780
How can we release?

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:35.830
How can put out
new functionality?

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:37.860
And as you know, change
is the biggest thing

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:39.270
that affects reliability.

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:41.190
So balancing between
those two things

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:44.130
is the main challenge of SRE
and why they've developed

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:46.830
the culture they have.

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.510
So I'm not going to
do a whole SRE talk.

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:51.120
That would be an
hour on its own.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:53.310
But I'll say from
the Stackdriver side,

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:56.226
what have we observed about SRE
that's special and different?

00:15:56.226 --> 00:15:57.600
And what are the
ingredients that

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.070
are needed to pull together?

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:03.380
So the first piece is culture.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:06.630
SREs aren't running around with
a pager playing whack-a-mole

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.010
with problems all day.

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:12.970
That would just not scale, and
they wouldn't be happy, either.

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:16.500
In fact, most
on-call shifts only

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:19.320
result in a couple
of pages a week.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:22.320
What are SREs doing,
then, before an incident?

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:25.440
They're working on automation,
automating all the monitoring,

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:30.080
automating all data collection,
automating all of the responses

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:31.820
to simple problems
that can be handled

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.760
without human intervention.

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:37.160
They're also helping the
application developers

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:39.082
engineer the reliability
into the product.

00:16:39.082 --> 00:16:40.790
They do the production
readiness reviews.

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:44.600
They say whether something is
ready to go live at huge scale.

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:46.910
They say whether it's really
worthy of having the pager

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:50.010
taken by the SRE team.

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:53.040
They're also responsible for,
when there is an incident,

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.810
the incident response process.

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:57.180
We won't talk about that
here, but that's in the book.

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:01.170
It's a really interesting,
scalable, predictable system

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:04.319
in the face of what
could be chaotic events.

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:07.260
They're responsible for the
blameless postmortems, which

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:09.660
are really important
because you're never

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:12.630
going to keep improving
reliability and agility

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:17.880
if you're not able to
look back at problems

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:20.369
and see what caused them
without people being afraid

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:23.640
of some kind of repercussions.

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.430
Now the most important part
of what the SREs work on

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:29.400
is SLOs, the service
level objectives.

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:31.410
That drives everything
we do and drives

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:33.462
how we think about reliability.

00:17:33.462 --> 00:17:34.920
I'm not going to
describe that here

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:36.870
because there's a slide
and a feature that

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:39.180
will go into that in
detail and apply that

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:43.270
to the serverless products.

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:45.790
Now if you're an
SRE team, and you're

00:17:45.790 --> 00:17:49.450
outnumbered by over 10 to 1,
and everything you're doing

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:52.450
is data driven, particularly
SLO work, imagine

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:55.326
a conversation where you go to
all these engineers and say,

00:17:55.326 --> 00:17:57.700
I want you to give me this
metric, this metric, this log,

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:58.790
this thing, that thing.

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:04.270
And I you to do it and keep
up with my thinking on how

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:06.310
monitoring should evolve.

00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:07.960
It'd be a short
conversation, right?

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:11.470
It's pretty hard to get all
these people to give you

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:13.310
exactly what you want.

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:15.220
So at Google, what we
did is we baked this

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:16.870
into the infrastructure.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:21.550
When a Google Developer
deploys a service,

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:24.190
that service is well-defined.

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:25.570
It's discoverable.

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:27.370
It has an identity.

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:29.620
Things like quota
and permissions

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:31.870
are config on that system.

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:33.430
And there's automatic telemetry.

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:39.514
There's a baseline of
telemetry that every service

00:18:39.514 --> 00:18:41.680
has that comes from the
infrastructure the developer

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:43.240
doesn't have to worry about.

00:18:43.240 --> 00:18:45.650
This keeps the developers
very productive.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:47.890
And it keeps the
SREs full of the data

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:50.590
that they need and removes
the fundamental tension that

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:53.247
could be there if we
weren't careful about that.

00:18:53.247 --> 00:18:55.330
Now you don't all work at
Google and don't develop

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:56.830
on Google infrastructure.

00:18:56.830 --> 00:19:02.660
But we've baked these principles
into the serverless runtimes.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:08.050
So functions and App Engine and
the Serverless Add-On, the GKE,

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.870
all return standardized
metrics that

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:13.720
can be used in a standardized
way in the Stackdriver

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.000
dashboards that
we'll be showing.

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:19.180
We've also open sourced other
nifty things inside Google

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:21.140
that are pretty helpful.

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:24.100
So Stubby is our
Secure RPC mechanism.

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:26.080
That's open sourced to GRPC.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:30.280
And Census, our
open source library

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:36.980
for collecting metrics
and traces that's

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:38.290
been released as open census.

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:44.140
Now Google's a big place.

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:46.710
And so if every app
is automatically

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:50.100
getting all these signals sent,
that turns into a lot of stuff.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:52.770
So we have petabytes
of logs every day,

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:56.010
billions of monitored
resources, hundreds of billions

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:57.000
of time series.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:58.200
Where does that all go?

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:00.420
Well, you need a platform
that scales to handle that.

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:03.630
You need to be able to
store it, keep it safe,

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:05.456
query it, do analytics on it.

00:20:05.456 --> 00:20:07.080
And we're not going
to talk about this.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.705
It's not the topic
of the presentation.

00:20:08.705 --> 00:20:10.980
But note that that is the
bottom half of Stackdriver.

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:13.230
When you use Stackdriver,
you're putting your metrics,

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:16.230
your logs, your traces in
the same robust and scalable

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:19.124
systems that Google
engineers are.

00:20:19.124 --> 00:20:21.040
The last piece, and this
is where we're really

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:22.600
going to focus, is the tools.

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:24.520
It's kind of the
tip of the iceberg.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:26.230
Everything that I
talked about before

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:28.620
is that giant iceberg
under the water.

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:30.490
What's above the
water is the tools.

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:34.270
How do I implement this
operational culture,

00:20:34.270 --> 00:20:37.360
taking advantage of all this
data and doing useful things?

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:40.180
And that's what we're going
to go into detail about

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:44.800
and demonstrate for you
is Stackdriver front end.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:49.970
So what tools are available for
serverless from Stackdriver?

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:52.040
So what are the columns?

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:53.660
First is monitoring.

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.310
This is basically your
metrics, whether it's

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:58.310
coming from the infrastructure
or custom metrics

00:20:58.310 --> 00:20:59.540
coming from the app.

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:00.800
It can be uptime checks.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:02.660
It can be log counts.

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:05.420
But all of these
metrics are stored.

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:07.190
They're queryable.

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:09.770
You can set alerts and
notifications on them,

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:12.560
create incidents when
they go out of whack.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.260
And so that's the core
piece that you expect

00:21:15.260 --> 00:21:17.590
from monitoring solution.

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:19.690
Then there's logging
and error reporting.

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:23.500
Logging is collection of logs,
error reports or special logs

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:26.980
that are tied to stack
traces and often times

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:29.630
you want to do
special analytics on.

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:32.710
Lastly is application
performance management.

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:37.150
That is our tracing
product, distributed trace,

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:38.230
we'll talk about.

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:42.400
Our continuous profiler,
which helps you manage costs.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:45.026
And the live debugger,
which lets you

00:21:45.026 --> 00:21:46.900
figure out the state of
your application down

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:49.720
to the variable level
without pausing, redeploying,

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:51.670
or crashing the app.

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:53.200
As you can see,
green check marks

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:55.360
all across the board
for App Engine.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:57.712
We've supported that
for quite a long time.

00:21:57.712 --> 00:21:59.920
In fact, a lot of these
products supported App Engine

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:03.690
before they supported
anything else.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:07.680
Cloud Functions monitoring,
logging, error reporting work,

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:08.910
no question.

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:12.060
The APM tools also
work, but some

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:14.640
of the libraries
and instrumentation

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.310
need to be optimized
for the function world.

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:19.860
So definitely encourage
people to try it out.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:22.050
And it may or may not
be ready for production

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:25.890
for your functions, depending.

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:28.440
Lastly, GKE
Serverless Add-On on.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:30.780
The Stackdriver team
and the serverless teams

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:32.970
are working very, very
closely to make sure

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:35.820
that these all work by the
time we go to public beta.

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:37.890
As of the alpha state,
it's still in progress.

00:22:41.030 --> 00:22:41.530
All right.

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:43.630
So let's look at this in action.

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:49.310
So basically we
have an application,

00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:52.550
and that application, we're
going to do things to it.

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:54.710
And the demos will show
how the monitoring features

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:56.220
come into play.

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:57.980
But Steren wrote
this, so let's let

00:22:57.980 --> 00:22:59.765
him describe the
architecture to us.

00:22:59.765 --> 00:23:01.640
STEREN GIANNINI: Let's
see what the app does,

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:04.290
and then I will explain
you how it's done.

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.170
So simply this is a website.

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:08.180
The title is Web Page Watcher.

00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:10.730
So what this website
does is that you

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:14.030
submit it a URL of a web page,
and then it will watch it

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:16.860
for changes, visual changes.

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:21.260
So for example, on
this test example app,

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:25.700
I wrote a static web
page that from when

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:27.110
the button changed color.

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:30.260
And as you see, my web
page watcher website

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.240
captured those two
what we call keyframes.

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:37.380
So there was a change, they
were captured visually.

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:38.870
So let's switch
back to the slides

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:43.780
to see how we built that
using serverless products.

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:46.940
So the first thing to know is
that every five minutes, there

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:48.890
is Cloud Scheduler,
which is going

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:51.620
to trigger another service.

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:54.610
And the service is what we
call All Task Scheduler.

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:59.180
So the Task Scheduler will
query the database for web pages

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:00.210
to watch.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:04.520
So the database will return OK,
I have this set of web pages.

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:05.920
Go watch them.

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:08.550
The scheduler for each
of those web pages

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:12.590
will create an event
in Cloud PubSub,

00:24:12.590 --> 00:24:15.600
and this event will be take
a screenshot of that page.

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:19.930
So one event per
screenshot to take.

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:22.510
This is the only
job of that service.

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:26.410
The second service has only
one job, taking screenshots

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:29.080
of the web page it gets.

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:31.832
So this other service is
written in App Engine.

00:24:31.832 --> 00:24:33.790
We could have written a
Cloud function for that

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:35.770
because both App
Engine and Cloud

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:38.710
Functions support
all of the libraries

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:41.350
that you need to
run Headless Chrome.

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:43.990
So the screenshot
service is running Chrome

00:24:43.990 --> 00:24:45.830
without a visual interface.

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:48.880
But it's rendering a web
page, taking a screenshot,

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:49.750
and that's it.

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:51.310
That's the only job
of that service.

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:52.060
It's super simple.

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:54.140
Literally hundreds line of code.

00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:57.910
I'm not going to show them,
but very simple to write.

00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:02.530
Once it took a screenshot, it
will store it in Cloud Storage.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:04.010
Simple.

00:25:04.010 --> 00:25:08.480
And anytime something is added
to that Cloud Storage bucket,

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:11.710
an event is triggered,
and this event

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:14.330
will trigger a Cloud Function.

00:25:14.330 --> 00:25:17.390
This Cloud function
also has only one job.

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:22.130
It's comparing the new
file with a reference file.

00:25:22.130 --> 00:25:27.930
And if there is a difference, it
will store this new screenshot

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:31.224
as the new reference, and it
will capture it as a keyframe.

00:25:31.224 --> 00:25:32.640
So here, if there
is a difference,

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:36.850
it will keep the new image
that it just received.

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:39.280
That's basically what's
happening every five minutes.

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:41.860
This is configured
with Cloud Scheduler.

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:44.770
And then when I open my
Web Page Watcher website

00:25:44.770 --> 00:25:47.160
as a user, what I'm doing
is I'm hitting a front end

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:50.560
service that is regular
website service that queries,

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:54.100
of course, the database
to look for the web pages

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:55.240
that we are tracking.

00:25:55.240 --> 00:25:58.830
And also, when I look at the
details of one of those web

00:25:58.830 --> 00:26:02.410
pages, it will query the
Cloud storage to return

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:04.530
the keyframes of that web page.

00:26:04.530 --> 00:26:08.480
So you see four services, four
miscroservices, each of them

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:10.010
super, super simple.

00:26:10.010 --> 00:26:13.290
But brought together, they are
doing something very complex

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:15.330
in a very reliable way.

00:26:15.330 --> 00:26:17.580
And so this is all demo app.

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:20.830
And now Jay is going to go
over some different scenario

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:25.780
for monitoring and
troubleshooting this app.

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:27.310
So what you should
know is that I

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:31.580
did a release of that
application 30 minutes ago.

00:26:31.580 --> 00:26:33.302
So let's see if this
release is healthy

00:26:33.302 --> 00:26:35.260
JAY JUDKOWITZ: I'm sure
it's going to be great.

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:41.380
So we're going to talk
about two features that

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:43.910
are going to come into
play in the first demo.

00:26:43.910 --> 00:26:48.080
The first feature
is a SLO feature.

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:50.020
So like I said, I deferred
earlier that we're

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:52.800
going to dive into this.

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:58.300
SREs, like I said,
they don't chase around

00:26:58.300 --> 00:27:02.200
problems playing whack-a-mole
with pager all day

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:04.560
because, again, the
toil would be too high

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:06.460
and the scale wouldn't be there.

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:09.070
What they really
worry about is what

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:11.270
affects the customer directly.

00:27:11.270 --> 00:27:13.490
So let's look at a
very trivial example.

00:27:13.490 --> 00:27:15.360
What if the CPU runs hot?

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:17.250
Well, that could
affect the customer.

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:20.160
But we've done the
obvious automation there.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:21.910
We've implemented auto scaling.

00:27:21.910 --> 00:27:23.620
It scales out.

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:26.660
The CPU drops, and if there was
any degradation of performance,

00:27:26.660 --> 00:27:27.870
it goes away.

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:29.370
There's no point
in paging somebody.

00:27:29.370 --> 00:27:31.780
It works, right?

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:34.550
But no matter how much
automation you do,

00:27:34.550 --> 00:27:36.900
no matter how much thought
you put into the system,

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:38.440
you always make it
better and better

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:40.930
but you never get rid
of every last problem.

00:27:40.930 --> 00:27:43.450
And so at some point,
there's something

00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:46.270
you didn't automate away
and it affects the user.

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:48.120
And that's what
the SLIs are about

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:50.456
is quantifying the
effect to the user.

00:27:50.456 --> 00:27:51.830
So what does the
user care about?

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:52.996
Do they care about your CPU?

00:27:52.996 --> 00:27:54.430
No, not at all.

00:27:54.430 --> 00:27:55.430
What do they care about?

00:27:55.430 --> 00:27:58.330
They care about two
obvious examples.

00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:00.190
Availability.

00:28:00.190 --> 00:28:04.130
What percentage of my calls
come back with a 200 response?

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:05.410
They care about latency.

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:07.660
Let's say a particular
service works well up

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:10.030
to 300 milliseconds
response time.

00:28:10.030 --> 00:28:14.500
So I care what
percentage of my requests

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:17.010
come back in 300
milliseconds or less.

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:18.010
So those are indicators.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:21.700
Those percentages are the
service level indicators.

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:24.280
Once you know what
it is you're watching

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:26.380
and what it is your
users care about,

00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:28.330
you put an objective on that.

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:30.730
And that objective is
sort of the promise you're

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:34.000
making to yourself, whether you
externalize the promise or not,

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.680
saying, this is how I
know my thing is working.

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:42.730
For example, I might
want 99.9% 200 responses.

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:46.360
Or I might want 99% of
my calls to come back

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:48.705
in under 300 milliseconds.

00:28:48.705 --> 00:28:50.830
Now you notice in those
examples I threw out there,

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:52.750
I didn't use the number 100.

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:54.980
And there are a couple
of good reasons for that.

00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:58.720
One is if you needed 100%,
you'd have zero flexibility

00:28:58.720 --> 00:28:59.590
to do anything.

00:28:59.590 --> 00:29:02.470
And any change anywhere
that broke anything

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:03.750
would break your promise.

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:06.400
So 100 doesn't often make sense.

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:09.820
But second, people are
pretty good at writing

00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:11.530
apps that are resilient.

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:12.700
You get a 500?

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:16.380
Retry, it'll probably work.

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:19.270
Web services, like if
you go to a web page

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:21.910
and 99 out of 100 times
it loads satisfactorily,

00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:25.120
and the last time it
doesn't load satisfactorily,

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:27.010
you'll be OK.

00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:31.780
So having a non-100% SLO is
a really, really important

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:33.050
concept.

00:29:33.050 --> 00:29:35.170
Now when you subtract
the objective

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:39.400
from 100, what you're left
with is the error budget.

00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:42.580
That is, how many calls
you're allowed to have that,

00:29:42.580 --> 00:29:44.720
in an availability
case, that aren't 200,

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:49.330
or in the latency case, that
are slower than you'd like.

00:29:49.330 --> 00:29:51.680
And by the way, there's other
SLOs and SLIs out there.

00:29:51.680 --> 00:29:54.640
I'm just going over
the most common ones.

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:56.530
And the error budget
is critical thing.

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:59.740
SREs really, really,
really focus on this

00:29:59.740 --> 00:30:06.400
because if your error budget
is low, or below zero,

00:30:06.400 --> 00:30:08.196
you should stop adding features.

00:30:08.196 --> 00:30:09.820
You should start
fixing technical debt.

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:11.890
You should hold off release.

00:30:11.890 --> 00:30:16.530
You should fix the system
and make it more reliable.

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:18.800
What if the error
budget is super high?

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:21.200
Well, you know
crank up the dial.

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:23.840
Say, hey, we're not
experimenting enough.

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:26.807
We're giving users more
reliability than they need.

00:30:26.807 --> 00:30:28.640
And that seems like it
would be a good thing

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:29.639
except for two problems.

00:30:29.639 --> 00:30:31.510
One, they could
start relying on that

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:33.250
and you may not be
able to maintain that.

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:34.400
Or two, you may
not be giving them

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:36.530
the features they want because
your customers want reliability

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:37.820
and they want features.

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:39.620
So make it as
reliable as they need,

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:41.420
and then get as many
features in as can.

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:45.650
Spend your budget,
your money budget.

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:51.200
And then what if your SLO is
OK, you haven't broken it,

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:53.120
but it's dropping precipitously?

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:54.740
Well, that's when
you page somebody.

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:57.530
If you have a precipitous
drop in error budget

00:30:57.530 --> 00:31:01.410
in an SLO for an indicator that
a customer really cares about,

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:05.000
that's when the SRE gets woken
up at 2:00 in the morning.

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:07.220
Now I've grayed out
this third concept, SLA,

00:31:07.220 --> 00:31:08.900
because it's not
really an SRE concept,

00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:11.330
but it's a business concept,
and people asked about it

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:12.950
in this context.

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:18.260
SLAs are when you give some kind
of guarantee to your customers,

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:19.850
maybe like I'll
give you a refund

00:31:19.850 --> 00:31:21.310
if I don't meet this SLO.

00:31:24.226 --> 00:31:25.600
By and large,
again, like I said,

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:27.500
not necessarily an SRE concept.

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:32.030
Obviously the advice here is if
you have an SLO tied to an SLA,

00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:34.460
you should probably have a
much stricter SLO internally

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:36.800
that you're not publishing
because you certainly

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:40.550
don't want your finance
and legal to be the ones

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:42.920
notified the first time
something goes wrong.

00:31:42.920 --> 00:31:44.510
You want to work
really, really hard

00:31:44.510 --> 00:31:46.910
to keep customer
happiness high, and then

00:31:46.910 --> 00:31:50.060
have your money back
guarantee at a higher

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:51.502
level of dysfunction.

00:31:54.334 --> 00:31:56.583
So feature number one is
going to be that SLO feature.

00:31:56.583 --> 00:32:00.090
Feature number two is an
integrated service dashboard.

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:03.230
So Stackdriver has
a lot of pieces,

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:05.919
and I kind of rattled
them off before.

00:32:05.919 --> 00:32:07.960
And they're all really
awesome, and independently

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:10.060
they all add a lot of value.

00:32:10.060 --> 00:32:12.810
But they're all in different
pages, different domains.

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:14.970
I have a dashboard page.

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:16.380
I have an incident page.

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:17.460
I have a log page.

00:32:17.460 --> 00:32:20.660
I have an error report
page, a trace page.

00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:23.300
But if I'm monitoring a
particular service, what

00:32:23.300 --> 00:32:26.450
I really want to know is,
what are all of the signals

00:32:26.450 --> 00:32:28.340
that I have for
that one service,

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:30.170
and put those all
in one place, and be

00:32:30.170 --> 00:32:33.560
able to have an integrated
troubleshooting experience that

00:32:33.560 --> 00:32:38.570
goes from I have an SLO
problem to here are my metrics,

00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:41.040
to here are my logs, and
go deeper and deeper.

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:44.250
And so that's what
we've put together.

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:46.550
So let's do Demo One.

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:49.460
Let's have a bird's eye
view of microservice

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:53.020
and see what's going on here.

00:32:53.020 --> 00:32:59.270
So what we're looking at
is the screenshot service.

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:00.610
You can see the name here.

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:02.450
Here you can see the timeline.

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:04.580
I can zoom in to
a particular time.

00:33:04.580 --> 00:33:06.570
We'll show that
later in the demo.

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:10.130
We have an incident
history, event history.

00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:12.290
And like I said, we're
going to start with the SLOs

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:15.140
because the SLOs are
what we're managing to.

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:16.550
We're not managing
CPU or memory.

00:33:16.550 --> 00:33:18.650
We're managing to the SLO.

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:22.010
And we have an SLO that says
we want 90% availability

00:33:22.010 --> 00:33:24.517
in the last seven rolling days.

00:33:24.517 --> 00:33:26.350
And as you can see,
we're doing pretty well.

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:27.620
We had a big hiccup yesterday.

00:33:27.620 --> 00:33:30.699
I guess Steren and I
were practicing the demo.

00:33:30.699 --> 00:33:33.240
And we were doing really well
and there was this little blip.

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:35.350
I'm not sure what that
was, but blips happen.

00:33:35.350 --> 00:33:37.610
It's a small one
and we survived it.

00:33:37.610 --> 00:33:41.600
But something bad is
happening right now.

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:43.820
We're definitely
not getting 90%.

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:49.800
We're something like 60%
of successful responses.

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:52.970
And the error budget,
accordingly, is low.

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:56.360
So we've been playing with
this over the last week

00:33:56.360 --> 00:33:57.890
and we're under zero.

00:33:57.890 --> 00:34:00.280
If it really looked
like this in real life,

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:03.230
everybody would be all hands
on deck for reliability surge.

00:34:03.230 --> 00:34:05.810
But right now you can
see the error budget,

00:34:05.810 --> 00:34:09.736
even though it was below zero,
it's dropping even further.

00:34:09.736 --> 00:34:11.860
And then we can look at
the metrics for the service

00:34:11.860 --> 00:34:14.120
and say what's going on here?

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:19.030
So this is a lot of
information here.

00:34:19.030 --> 00:34:24.570
But what's cool is we have
this group buy and filter

00:34:24.570 --> 00:34:25.300
functionality.

00:34:25.300 --> 00:34:28.659
So for example, Steren
mentioned he did an upgrade.

00:34:28.659 --> 00:34:33.000
So let's group by version ID.

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:37.710
And so now you could see we
have all these different--

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:39.476
we have the new and the old.

00:34:39.476 --> 00:34:41.100
But there's still a
lot of lines there.

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:44.650
I just want to know about
the 500 response codes.

00:34:44.650 --> 00:34:47.070
So I can filter to that.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:50.650
Response code 500.

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:52.090
And here we go.

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:59.640
So this is the new one
and this is the old one.

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:03.810
So like I said, the old one
had that one little blip.

00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:04.800
But that's not bad.

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:06.270
That went away.

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:11.699
The new one is giving us
errors, and more and more errors

00:35:11.699 --> 00:35:12.240
all the time.

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:13.110
And this is not good.

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:14.526
It's burning through
error budget.

00:35:14.526 --> 00:35:18.420
So I could worry about
debugging the code or whatnot,

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:20.490
but right now I just
want to roll back.

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:26.630
So I go over to App Engine, and
I can go to the old version,

00:35:26.630 --> 00:35:28.370
and I can migrate
all the traffic.

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:36.080
And if we come back here, what
we'll find in a minute or two

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:41.450
is that we'll see
the errors go down,

00:35:41.450 --> 00:35:43.080
and we'll come back
to the SLO page,

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:47.990
and we'll see the SLIs going up.

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:50.361
But for the sake of--

00:35:50.361 --> 00:35:51.860
we can come back
to that at the end.

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:53.780
You can see already
the SLIs starting

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:55.970
to flatten out pretty quickly.

00:35:55.970 --> 00:35:58.880
So that is the first demo.

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:02.300
And let's see what
we're going to do next.

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:09.810
So features that are going
to show up in the next demo

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:11.617
include distributed tracing.

00:36:11.617 --> 00:36:13.200
So just by show of
hands, who here has

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:16.086
used distributed tracing before?

00:36:16.086 --> 00:36:17.030
No?

00:36:17.030 --> 00:36:17.590
OK.

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:18.190
One person.

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:18.689
Awesome.

00:36:18.689 --> 00:36:20.860
OK, so we're going to display
it for everybody else.

00:36:20.860 --> 00:36:25.562
Like Steren said, if I
have microservice-based

00:36:25.562 --> 00:36:27.520
application-- he showed
one with, I don't know,

00:36:27.520 --> 00:36:28.930
five microservices.

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:31.840
But what if you have
tens, or some people

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:34.090
have even hundreds
of microservices.

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:37.714
If something is
slow, why is it slow?

00:36:37.714 --> 00:36:39.130
Do you just start
at the front end

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:40.864
and try to figure
out all the way back

00:36:40.864 --> 00:36:42.280
where the slowness
is coming from?

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:44.490
That'll take you all day.

00:36:44.490 --> 00:36:52.990
What distributed traces
do is they sample the code

00:36:52.990 --> 00:36:56.750
and they basically say,
OK, for this transaction,

00:36:56.750 --> 00:36:58.130
I'm going to trace this one.

00:36:58.130 --> 00:36:59.713
I'm going to find
out how much latency

00:36:59.713 --> 00:37:01.859
came from this
microservice, how much came

00:37:01.859 --> 00:37:04.400
from this microservice, how much
came from this microservice.

00:37:04.400 --> 00:37:05.358
And it draws a picture.

00:37:05.358 --> 00:37:07.520
It ties it together
into a waterfall.

00:37:07.520 --> 00:37:10.060
And then you're able to
see, just at a glance,

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:11.360
where's the slow span?

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:14.370
And when you figure out
which span is the slow one,

00:37:14.370 --> 00:37:18.050
you can then go into other
tools like we'll talk about next

00:37:18.050 --> 00:37:19.790
to figure out why.

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:22.050
So distributed
tracing is a really,

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:24.410
really, really powerful
tool for figuring out

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:26.780
what's wrong with
your microservices.

00:37:26.780 --> 00:37:29.270
The instrumentation for
that is open census.

00:37:29.270 --> 00:37:32.410
And that is a vendor
agnostic instrumentation.

00:37:32.410 --> 00:37:34.910
And we're working with a lot
of our partners and competitors

00:37:34.910 --> 00:37:38.150
on this to make sure that
when you can get tracing,

00:37:38.150 --> 00:37:39.960
it's not just tracing
for Stackdriver,

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:41.690
but it's tracing for whoever.

00:37:41.690 --> 00:37:44.619
So if you switch tools,
maybe as an ops person,

00:37:44.619 --> 00:37:46.410
you're switching tools
and that's a bummer.

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:49.000
But for your developers,
they don't care.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:51.890
They still have census.

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:53.861
Now like I said, what
about why is it slow?

00:37:53.861 --> 00:37:55.610
So once you figure out
which span is slow,

00:37:55.610 --> 00:37:56.810
you can go to the profiler.

00:37:56.810 --> 00:38:00.670
And the profiler tells
you continuously,

00:38:00.670 --> 00:38:03.500
also through sampling,
which functions in your code

00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:06.399
are taking up CPU, which
ones are taking up time.

00:38:06.399 --> 00:38:07.940
You could figure
out who your problem

00:38:07.940 --> 00:38:12.050
child is down to the function
level within the microservice.

00:38:12.050 --> 00:38:14.620
And so that's a
really powerful tool

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:16.810
that we've released recently.

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:20.900
So let's look into
a performance issue.

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:29.396
So again-- sorry, just
on the last demo--

00:38:29.396 --> 00:38:30.520
we'll come back at the end.

00:38:30.520 --> 00:38:34.240
I just want to make
sure for time that we--

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:36.490
so we're at different
service now.

00:38:36.490 --> 00:38:38.554
We're at default service.

00:38:38.554 --> 00:38:39.970
So on the default
service, you can

00:38:39.970 --> 00:38:42.550
see again we wanted
less than half

00:38:42.550 --> 00:38:45.040
a second on 95% of the calls.

00:38:45.040 --> 00:38:47.920
We were doing good except
for our big test there.

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:54.760
And again, the rollout has
caused us a problem here.

00:38:54.760 --> 00:38:58.070
So we can go into the metrics
and see what's going on.

00:38:58.070 --> 00:39:02.410
And this heat map
here is showing us

00:39:02.410 --> 00:39:05.140
that this is where the
latency generally was hottest,

00:39:05.140 --> 00:39:07.040
but now it's up here.

