00:42:35.780 --> 00:42:39.430
Now going back to the
last demo, the tools

00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:45.290
we will use in that demo are
on Stackdriver Error Reporting.

00:42:45.290 --> 00:42:47.990
So Stackdriver Error
Reporting is automatically

00:42:47.990 --> 00:42:52.080
monitoring your logs for
some application errors.

00:42:52.080 --> 00:42:54.920
So for example, when there is a
crash, most of the time in your

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:57.730
logs you will see a stack
trace, an exception.

00:42:57.730 --> 00:43:00.090
And so Stackdriver,
in real time,

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:03.200
is looking at all of your
logs looking for crashes.

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:06.320
When it finds a crash,
it will capture it.

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:09.330
And when it finds the
same kind of crash,

00:43:09.330 --> 00:43:11.750
it will group them
together so that

00:43:11.750 --> 00:43:16.400
what you will focus on when you
open Error Reporting are groups

00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:19.490
of errors, not single
entries, but really they

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:22.340
are grouped
meaningfully together.

00:43:22.340 --> 00:43:23.880
So it's all real time.

00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:26.870
And the way to get it
is simply via logging.

00:43:26.870 --> 00:43:30.200
So that means that on all
of our serverless products,

00:43:30.200 --> 00:43:32.580
because you get
logging out of the box,

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:37.000
you get also error
reporting out of the box.

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:40.420
And the second tool I
will use in that last demo

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:43.050
is Stackdriver Debugger.

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:46.420
So Stackdriver Debugger
is an amazing technology.

00:43:46.420 --> 00:43:50.560
I told you before I'm not
able to SSH in my VMs.

00:43:50.560 --> 00:43:54.070
But that doesn't mean I
cannot inspect the state

00:43:54.070 --> 00:43:55.690
of my instances.

00:43:55.690 --> 00:43:57.850
And basically what
Debugger allows you to do

00:43:57.850 --> 00:44:01.000
is to inspect the state
of a running application

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:03.070
without slowing it down.

00:44:03.070 --> 00:44:04.770
So it's not like a breakpoint.

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:06.380
It doesn't stop the execution.

00:44:06.380 --> 00:44:07.210
No, no, no.

00:44:07.210 --> 00:44:10.450
It's when you put a
snapshot, as we call them,

00:44:10.450 --> 00:44:13.420
it will snapshot the
entire state of the app

00:44:13.420 --> 00:44:15.380
and then continue the execution.

00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:18.520
But you will have
visibility into the state

00:44:18.520 --> 00:44:19.970
of your application.

00:44:19.970 --> 00:44:21.560
So we have snapshots.

00:44:21.560 --> 00:44:25.430
And we have one of my favorite
feature, which is logpoints.

00:44:25.430 --> 00:44:29.770
So how many times did you just
redeploy a new version just

00:44:29.770 --> 00:44:32.350
by adding some log statements
because you couldn't figure out

00:44:32.350 --> 00:44:35.080
what was wrong on production?

00:44:35.080 --> 00:44:38.770
And what Debugger
Logpoints allow you to do

00:44:38.770 --> 00:44:43.690
are simply to add log statements
dynamically in your code

00:44:43.690 --> 00:44:46.300
without having to redeploy
because under the hood,

00:44:46.300 --> 00:44:49.850
we are using the exact same
technology as for snapshots,

00:44:49.850 --> 00:44:51.760
which are that
every time the code

00:44:51.760 --> 00:44:56.080
will execute at that point,
we will snapshot the state,

00:44:56.080 --> 00:45:00.830
and we will print a log
entry into your logs.

00:45:00.830 --> 00:45:03.910
So you can dynamically add
a log statement to your code

00:45:03.910 --> 00:45:06.310
without redeploying.

00:45:06.310 --> 00:45:09.700
To setup Stackdriver Debugger
you have to import a library.

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:12.430
This library, when you
are not using Debugger,

00:45:12.430 --> 00:45:15.370
has no impact on your app,
and when you're using it,

00:45:15.370 --> 00:45:17.340
has very, very low impact.

00:45:17.340 --> 00:45:20.230
So I recommend to
import it in case,

00:45:20.230 --> 00:45:24.950
for the future, something needs
to be diagnosed in production.

00:45:24.950 --> 00:45:28.060
So my last demo will be using
Error Reporting and Debugger

00:45:28.060 --> 00:45:31.180
to understand an application
error based on an availability

